sections.body:
Every stanza of the poem above builds toward its last four lines, worth reading again on their own before anything else, because the whole piece is a run-up to one demand:

> Think on me too, O Mother, who wrest my soul to serve you
> In strange and fearful ways beyond your encircling waters;
> None but you can know my heart, its tears and sacrifice;
> None, but you, repay.

That is not quite a prayer. It reads more like a claim submitted for payment: a soldier telling his country that she alone is qualified to assess what she owes him, and that she owes it. Ivor Gurney had been a private in the Gloucestershire Regiment, service number 3895, since [9 February 1915](https://war.web.ox.ac.uk/ivor-gurney), and by the time [Severn and Somme](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/63895/63895-h/63895-h.htm) reached print that November he had gone through trench duty near Laventie and the Somme, a wound to the arm that sent him to hospital at Rouen, and a gas attack near the Passchendaele Ridge that ended his war. Whatever the dreadful service of the poem's first line means, he had earned the standing to describe it. What the poem could not know in 1917 is that the tears and sacrifice its early readers would end up believing in had a longer, stranger history than the one it tells.
